# Test: ze starts a second time over a tunnel netdev its own first run created
# Exercises: iface OnConfigure -> applyConfig(cfg, nil, b) -> CreateTunnel ->
#            RTM_NEWLINK EEXIST -> the kept-netdev branch
#            (internal/component/iface/config_apply.go, D-5/D-6/D-7)
# VALIDATES: the second start applies cleanly and KEEPS the device the first
#            start made. The kernel ifindex is carried from the first run to the
#            second and compared, so "the netdev survives" means the same
#            device, not merely a device with that name.
# PREVENTS:  the restart failure this branch exists for. netlink.LinkAdd sends
#            NLM_F_CREATE|NLM_F_EXCL (vendor/github.com/vishvananda/netlink/
#            link_linux.go), nothing in internal/component/iface deletes tunnels
#            when the daemon stops, and OnConfigure runs applyConfig with a nil
#            previous at EVERY plugin start. So the second start met EEXIST over
#            ze's own netdev, failed the whole interface apply, and the
#            fail-closed startup cascade then refused to start the daemon.
#
# Why unit evidence cannot reach this obligation: every unit test of the branch
# runs against fakeBackend (internal/component/iface/config_test.go), whose
# EEXIST answer and link-type read-back are hand-written. They prove the branch
# reacts to what the fake says. Only a real kernel says whether RTM_NEWLINK
# answers EEXIST for a tunnel a dead process left behind, and whether that
# netdev is still there to answer for.
#
# Why the assertion discriminates:
#  - Revert the kept-netdev branch and the second apply fails on `file exists`,
#    the startup cascade stops the daemon, the pass 2 observer never runs, and
#    the seq=4 daemon does not exit 0.
#  - Replace "keep" with "delete and re-create" and the netdev is a NEW device:
#    the ifindex differs and pass 2 fails on the comparison, not on absence.
#  - Break the FIRST run instead and pass 1 fails on its own reading of
#    /sys/class/net, so a second run that merely creates the tunnel itself
#    cannot carry the test. Pass 1 recording nothing is a failure too, at the
#    seq=2 barrier: pass 2 comparing against a file pass 1 never wrote is the
#    one outcome this test must never call green.
#
# The two ze runs are sequential, not concurrent: cmd=stop with signal=term
# reaps the first daemon before the second starts (internal/test/runner/
# runner_exec_util.go, stopBackgroundProcess). The first run is a background
# command so the runner waits for daemon.ready, which cmd/ze/hub/main.go writes
# only after WaitForStartupComplete -- that is, after the interface apply.
#
# daemon.ready is NOT a barrier for the observer, which is why seq=2 exists.
# WaitForStartupComplete returns as soon as startupDone is closed, and the
# goroutine that closes it has only just SPAWNED the post-startup delivery:
# sendPostStartupToAll (internal/component/plugin/server/startup.go) hands each
# plugin its callback to a goroutine and deliberately does not wait, so nothing
# orders "the ready file exists" against "the observer has been told". The stop
# step could therefore reach the daemon while the pass 1 observer was still
# blocked in wait_for_post_startup, and pass 1 then recorded nothing at all.
# The seq=2 barrier holds the stop until the recorded ifindex is on disk, so
# pass 1 cannot reach the stop step without having written the file.
#
# The endpoint pair 192.0.2.211 -> 198.51.100.211 is this test's own. The kernel
# refuses a second tunnel of one driver on a pair it already holds whatever the
# device is named, and the suites share one VM.
# TestCITunnelEndpointsAreUniqueAcrossTests
# (internal/test/runner/tunnel_endpoint_lint_test.go) enforces that. The same
# interface name appears in both configs below and is one holder there, because
# one device answers for both runs.

# Creating links needs CAP_NET_ADMIN, not merely Linux (ai/rules/platform-linux.md).
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin



# Barrier: the compiled fixture polls restart-ifindex 121 times at 100ms.
# The file must be non-empty because pass 1 creates it before writing the
# kernel ifindex. The ~12s bound outlives the engine's 10s post-startup
# delivery timeout, so failure is reported here rather than as an outer timeout.

# First run's config. The interface stanza is byte-identical to the second
# run's: only the observer differs, so the restart applies one tunnel config
# twice.
stdin=boot:terminator=EOF_BOOT_CONF
plugin {
	external iface-tunnel-restart-boot {
		run "ze-test fixture plugin/iface-tunnel-restart-boot"
		encoder json
	}
}

interface {
	backend netlink

	tunnel trs0 {
		encapsulation {
			gre {
				local { ip 192.0.2.211; }
				remote { ip 198.51.100.211; }
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_BOOT_CONF

# Second run's config: same tunnel, the checking observer.
stdin=restart:terminator=EOF_RESTART_CONF
plugin {
	external iface-tunnel-restart-check {
		run "ze-test fixture plugin/iface-tunnel-restart-check"
		encoder json
	}
}

interface {
	backend netlink

	tunnel trs0 {
		encapsulation {
			gre {
				local { ip 192.0.2.211; }
				remote { ip 198.51.100.211; }
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_RESTART_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=boot:name=ze1
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze-test fixture plugin/iface-tunnel-restart-wait
cmd=stop:seq=3:name=ze1:signal=term
# 30s, not the 20s this carried before the seq=2 barrier: the barrier is allowed
# ~12s of its own, and the budget it sits inside has to leave room for that plus
# both daemon startups, or a barrier failure is reported as a bare timeout with
# the message it printed nowhere in the report.
cmd=foreground:seq=4:exec=ze -:stdin=restart:timeout=30s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=OK: tunnel restart pass 1 created trs0 ifindex
# The barrier's own line: proof the stop step ran AFTER the ifindex was on disk,
# rather than the barrier having been skipped.
expect=stdout:contains=OK: tunnel restart pass 1 recorded ifindex
expect=stderr:contains=OK: tunnel restart pass 2 kept trs0 same ifindex
# The symptom the kept-netdev branch removes. A second start that fails the
# interface apply logs it and takes the daemon down with it.
reject=stderr:pattern=file exists
reject=stderr:pattern=panic
